      <h1 class="blog-post__title">My AI reports stock prices to me twice a day. I just trade.</h1>
      <p class="blog-post__subtitle">I told my AI to track 鴻海 (Hon Hai) and report the price at 10:30 and 11:50 every day. No app to open, no alerts to dismiss — just the numbers I need, when I need them.</p>
      <div class="blog-post__meta">
        <span>April 2026</span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>2 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-post__hero-image">
      <img src="/images/blog-stock-price-alerts.png" alt="AI sending scheduled stock price reports via chat">
    </div>

    <div class="blog-post__content">
      <p>I trade Taiwanese stocks from Hong Kong. The market opens at 9:00 AM Taipei time, and I need to make decisions during the morning session — but I'm also working my day job. I can't sit there watching every tick.</p>

      <p>So I told my AI:</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>&ldquo;Help me report 鴻海's stock price twice daily at preset times, so I can do my trading when needed.&rdquo;</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>My AI asked a clarifying question — which stock, and what times? I said:</p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>&ldquo;鴻海 (2317.TW). After market close would be best — so 10:30 and 11:50 AM.&rdquo;</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>That was it. My AI set up an automated schedule. Now, twice a day, I get a message like this:</p>

      <div class="highlight-box">
        <p><strong>鴻海 (2317) — 2026/04/02 (三)</strong></p>
        <p><strong>收盤價:</strong> 193.0</p>
        <p><strong>跌 4.0 元</strong> (-2.03%)</p>
        <p><strong>開盤:</strong> 198.0 → <strong>最高:</strong> 199.0 → <strong>最低:</strong> 191.5</p>
        <p><strong>成交金額:</strong> 70.46 億</p>
        <p><strong>昨收:</strong> 197.0</p>
      </div>

      <p>Opening price, high, low, close, volume — everything I need to decide whether to buy, sell, or hold. Without opening any app.</p>

      <h2>Why this matters</h2>

      <p>Trading while holding a full-time job means you're always time-splitting. The old way:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Open a stock app (Yahoo Finance, 富途, etc.)</li>
        <li>Search for the ticker</li>
        <li>Check the numbers</li>
        <li>Decide if you need to act</li>
        <li>Repeat multiple times a day</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The new way: my AI proactively sends me the data. I read the message, make a decision, and move on. Takes 10 seconds instead of 10 minutes of app-switching.</p>

      <h2>Extending it</h2>

      <p>Once the first stock alert was running, it was easy to add more:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Multiple stocks</strong> — track a watchlist of 5-10 tickers, not just one</li>
        <li><strong>Conditional alerts</strong> — &ldquo;tell me if 鴻海 drops below 190 or breaks 200&rdquo;</li>
        <li><strong>Pre-market briefing</strong> — &ldquo;before market open, give me the ADR price and any overnight news&rdquo;</li>
        <li><strong>Weekly summary</strong> — &ldquo;every Friday close, send me a week-over-week comparison&rdquo;</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The AI doesn't just report numbers — it can add context. Like noting that 鴻海 has been dropping for the week and flagging the trend.</p>

      <h2>Who is this for?</h2>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Part-time traders</strong> — people who trade stocks but can't watch the market all day</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-border investors</strong> — trading Taiwan/Korea/US markets from Hong Kong</li>
        <li><strong>Portfolio watchers</strong> — anyone who wants to track specific stocks without living in a finance app</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The setup takes 5 minutes. After that, it runs automatically — every day, on schedule, no manual input needed.</p>
